1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,545
This tutorial shows how an organisation
starts its books

2
00:00:03,545 --> 00:00:06,697
in Miftah when it moves from another
system.

3
00:00:06,697 --> 00:00:09,454
Signed in as the Company Admin, open

4
00:00:09,454 --> 00:00:11,818
Accounting and choose Year End Closing.

5
00:00:11,818 --> 00:00:14,969
Palm Ridge's books start on 1 September
2025,

6
00:00:14,969 --> 00:00:17,726
and its fiscal year starts in January.

7
00:00:17,726 --> 00:00:22,060
The period before the books start is
locked, so nothing can

8
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:25,999
be posted into the months your old system
still owns.

9
00:00:25,999 --> 00:00:28,756
From here on, the Accountant takes over.

10
00:00:28,756 --> 00:00:31,120
Open Import Batches under One-time setup.

11
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:34,271
Contracts that are still running come
across from

12
00:00:34,271 --> 00:00:37,029
the cut-over workbook, with their
outstanding cheques.

13
00:00:37,029 --> 00:00:41,756
Posting a batch writes each contract's
journals on its own dates, and

14
00:00:41,756 --> 00:00:46,483
a batch can be reversed as a whole while
you check it.

15
00:00:46,483 --> 00:00:49,240
Palm Ridge's contracts were entered one by

16
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:51,998
one, so there are no batches here.

17
00:00:51,998 --> 00:00:53,573
Now open Opening Balances.

18
00:00:53,573 --> 00:00:57,513
The accounts that contracts produce, such
as rent receivable, post-dated

19
00:00:57,513 --> 00:01:01,452
cheques, advance rent and deposits, are
greyed out: they are

20
00:01:01,452 --> 00:01:05,391
worked out from the contracts, and you
never type them.

21
00:01:05,391 --> 00:01:09,724
Everything else is entered as it stood on
31 August 2025:

22
00:01:09,724 --> 00:01:14,057
250,000 in the Residences bank account,
180,000 in the Business Centre

23
00:01:14,057 --> 00:01:18,390
account, 12,600 owed to Coastline Facility
Services, and 417,400 of capital.

24
00:01:18,390 --> 00:01:22,724
Debits and credits both total 430,000, so
the difference is zero.

25
00:01:22,724 --> 00:01:27,451
Opening balances are the last step: once
they are posted, an import

26
00:01:27,451 --> 00:01:31,784
batch cannot be posted or reversed until
they are reversed first.

27
00:01:31,784 --> 00:01:35,329
In Journal Voucher, the opening balance
journal, OB, debits

28
00:01:35,329 --> 00:01:38,874
the two bank accounts with 250,000 and
180,000, and

29
00:01:38,874 --> 00:01:42,026
credits Coastline with 12,600 and Capital
with 417,400.

30
00:01:42,026 --> 00:01:43,601
The books open balanced.

31
00:01:43,601 --> 00:01:48,328
The 12,600 owed to Coastline is one real
invoice, so it is

32
00:01:48,328 --> 00:01:52,662
recorded under AP opening items, with its
number and due date.

33
00:01:52,662 --> 00:01:57,389
It was paid in October's payment run, and
the check shows the

34
00:01:57,389 --> 00:02:01,722
supplier's open items agreeing with its
opening balance, with no difference.

35
00:02:01,722 --> 00:02:03,298
Finally, open Cut-over reconciliation.

36
00:02:03,298 --> 00:02:06,843
For every account it compares Miftah's
balance, leaving out

37
00:02:06,843 --> 00:02:10,388
the opening journal, with the old system's
trial balance.

38
00:02:10,388 --> 00:02:13,933
The four rows that differ are exactly the
ones

39
00:02:13,933 --> 00:02:17,479
entered by hand; any account worked out
from contracts

40
00:02:17,479 --> 00:02:21,024
must show no difference, and the total is
zero.
